title: A running bash script is replaced by renaming a new file over it, never by editing it in place.
pid: 0ce54de3-b389-49ca-b49d-03064a56d7e0
body: |2-
  Bash reads a script a piece at a time as it runs, so an edit in place
  changes what a running loop does next, mid-function. The pacer runs
  for days. Write the new file beside the old, give it the old one's
  mode, and 'mv' it over: the running bash keeps reading the old file,
  and the next start reads the new one.
  Once it is renamed over, the new file may be edited in place: the
  running bash holds the old one open, and no longer sees the path.
